# Analysing a Castor EDC study with Explorer

```elixir
# To use checkouts instead of the published packages, set
# CASTOR_EDC_EXPLORER_PATH, CASTOR_EDC_KINO_PATH and CASTOR_EDC_PATH.
explorer = System.get_env("CASTOR_EDC_EXPLORER_PATH")
kino = System.get_env("CASTOR_EDC_KINO_PATH")
core = System.get_env("CASTOR_EDC_PATH")

Mix.install(
  [
    if(explorer,
      do: {:ex_castor_edc_explorer, path: explorer},
      else: {:ex_castor_edc_explorer, "~> 0.1.0"}
    ),
    if(kino, do: {:ex_castor_edc_kino, path: kino}, else: {:ex_castor_edc_kino, "~> 0.1.0"}),
    {:kino_explorer, "~> 0.1.25"}
  ] ++ if(core, do: [{:ex_castor_edc, path: core, override: true}], else: [])
)
```

## Introduction

This notebook loads a study's data into Explorer dataframes and takes a first look at it.
Every frame's dtypes come from the study's structure, not from the values it happens to hold.

## Connecting

Add your API credentials as Livebook secrets: `CASTOR_EDC_CLIENT_ID` and `CASTOR_EDC_CLIENT_SECRET`, which you can create on your [account settings page](https://data.castoredc.com/account/settings).
The connection cell below reads them from there, so they never appear in the notebook: pick the server your studies are on, and test the connection.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.CastorEDC.Kino.ConnectionCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir
client =
  CastorEDC.new(
    client_id: System.fetch_env!("LB_CASTOR_EDC_CLIENT_ID"),
    client_secret: System.fetch_env!("LB_CASTOR_EDC_CLIENT_SECRET"),
    base_url: "https://data.castoredc.com"
  )
```

Then pick the study to look at; the cell lists the studies the client can reach.

<!-- livebook:{"attrs":"eyJjbGllbnQiOiJjbGllbnQiLCJzdHVkeV9pZCI6IiIsInN0dWR5X25hbWUiOiIiLCJ2YXJpYWJsZSI6InN0dWR5X2lkIn0","chunks":null,"kind":"Elixir.CastorEDC.Kino.StudyCell","livebook_object":"smart_cell"} -->

```elixir

```

Explorer's queries, such as `filter` and `summarise`, are macros, so the notebook requires `Explorer.DataFrame` once.

```elixir
require Explorer.DataFrame
```

## The study's structure

`fields/2` has a row per field that holds values, in the order the study shows them: where it collects data, its type, and its option group.

```elixir
{:ok, fields} = CastorEDC.Explorer.fields(client, study_id)
fields
```

`option_groups/2` has a row per option, to join on `option_group_id`, though `data_points/3` can give the options' names itself (see below).

```elixir
{:ok, options} = CastorEDC.Explorer.option_groups(client, study_id)
options
```

## Participants and sites

```elixir
{:ok, participants} = CastorEDC.Explorer.participants(client, study_id)
{:ok, sites} = CastorEDC.Explorer.sites(client, study_id)

participants
|> Explorer.DataFrame.join(Explorer.DataFrame.select(sites, ["site_id", "name"]), on: "site_id", how: :left)
|> Explorer.DataFrame.group_by("name")
|> Explorer.DataFrame.summarise(participants: count(participant_id), mean_progress: mean(progress))
```

## Collected data

`participant_data/3` runs a batch export and reads it: a frame for the study's values, with a row per participant, and one for each repeating data and survey, with a row per instance.
It takes a few requests, however big the study is.

```elixir
{:ok, %{study: study, repeating_data: repeating_data, surveys: surveys}} =
  CastorEDC.Explorer.participant_data(client, study_id, missing_columns: true)

study
```

Values marked missing are nulls, with their codes (95 to 99) in the `<variable>_missing` columns.

```elixir
Map.keys(repeating_data) ++ Map.keys(surveys)
```

An export you already downloaded reads the same way, without running another: pass the `CastorEDC.Export` struct, or the path to its zip or the directory it was unzipped into, as `from:`.

```elixir
{:ok, export} = CastorEDC.Export.participant_data(client, study_id, format: :csv)
{:ok, %{study: study}} = CastorEDC.Explorer.participant_data(client, study_id, from: export)

Explorer.DataFrame.shape(study)
```

`data_points/3` reads the same values through the API instead, page by page, with a row per value, which suits filtering and pivoting.
With `labels: true`, a `label` column holds the names of the chosen options, as the user saw them, next to their values:

```elixir
{:ok, values} = CastorEDC.Explorer.data_points(client, study_id, labels: true)

values
|> Explorer.DataFrame.filter(not is_nil(label))
|> Explorer.DataFrame.group_by(["variable_name", "label"])
|> Explorer.DataFrame.summarise(count: count(participant_id))
```

Values marked missing have their code in `missing`:

```elixir
values
|> Explorer.DataFrame.filter(not is_nil(missing))
|> Explorer.DataFrame.group_by(["variable_name", "missing"])
|> Explorer.DataFrame.summarise(count: count(participant_id))
```

With `format: :wide`, it gives the same shape as `participant_data/3`, with a column per variable; `labels: true` puts the options' names in its radio and dropdown columns.

## Surveys, repeating data and queries

`survey_package_instances/2` has a row per survey package sent to (or started for) a participant, with its progress and when it was sent and finished:

```elixir
{:ok, packages} = CastorEDC.Explorer.survey_package_instances(client, study_id)

packages
|> Explorer.DataFrame.group_by(["survey_package_name", "status"])
|> Explorer.DataFrame.summarise(instances: count(survey_package_instance_id), mean_progress: mean(progress))
```

`repeating_data_instances/2` has a row per repeating data instance, such as each adverse event; their values are in the frames above, by `instance_id`:

```elixir
{:ok, instances} = CastorEDC.Explorer.repeating_data_instances(client, study_id)

instances
|> Explorer.DataFrame.filter(not archived)
|> Explorer.DataFrame.group_by("repeating_data_name")
|> Explorer.DataFrame.summarise(instances: count(repeating_data_instance_id), participants: n_distinct(participant_id))
```

`queries/2` has a row per query raised on a value; join it with `fields/2` for the field's name:

```elixir
{:ok, queries} = CastorEDC.Explorer.queries(client, study_id)

queries
|> Explorer.DataFrame.join(Explorer.DataFrame.select(fields, ["field_id", "variable_name"]), on: "field_id", how: :left)
|> Explorer.DataFrame.select(["participant_id", "variable_name", "status", "first_query_remark", "remarks", "created_on"])
```

## Anything else

`from_structs/1` makes a frame from any other result of the client, a list, page or stream of its structs, such as the study's visits.
Its dtypes come from the values, as nothing else describes them, and nested objects are left out.

```elixir
client
|> CastorEDC.Forms.stream_visits(study_id)
|> CastorEDC.Explorer.from_structs()
```
